      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ken Lund from Reno, Nevada, USA",
      "description": "Avalon, or Avalon Bay, is the only incorporated city on Santa Catalina Island of the California Channel Islands, and the southernmost city in Los Angeles County. The population was 3,728 at the 2010 census.\nAvalon was first settled in pre-modern times by members of the Gabrielino/Tongva tribe. In 1919, William Wrigley, Jr. gained control of Avalon. Wrigley oversaw much of the development of Avalon, including the construction of the landmark Catalina Casino.\nThe city remains primarily a resort community. Most of the waterfront is dominated by tourism-oriented businesses. The older parts of the town on the valley floor consist primarily of small houses and two and three-story buildings in various traditional architectural styles. There are also several large apartment complexes nestled in the hills on either side of the valley, so that they are not obvious in most postcard photos of Avalon.\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avalon,_California\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Text_of_Creative_Commons_...",
      "dateCreated": "2013-04-11 10:20",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
